			},
			"55": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "EXAMPLE: BPM-DISTORTION\n\nIn this example we've used the Transport Beat node as the \"beating heart\" of the patch.\nIt shoots off an Attack Release node which controls both the UV-Offset gain, which causes the image distortion. The Attack Release node is also used on the Color Offset node to distort the red and blue channel (in opposite directions!).\n\nThis example also uses the Transport Time node which simply gives you the amount of time, beats and bars that have passed since the transport started. In this patch it is used as a steady clock to animate the Fractal Noise.\n"
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 811.45037841796875,
					"width": 2162.567138671875,
					"x": -44.50693893432617188,
					"y": 1019.044921875
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffae7bff",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Group",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"6": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": true
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "BPM-SYNC \n\nIn this tutorial we're diving into the wonderful world of BPM-sycing your visuals.\nYou've learned about the Transport Bar in the fundamentals tutorials and if you followed the basic tutorials you should know how to work with triggers.\nIn this tutorial we're going to combine these two to achieve BPM synchronization.\n\nBPM stands for beats per minute and is the defining trait of how fast a song is.\nSynchronizing events in Wire to the BPM has the advantage of your visuals speeding up and slowing down when the song does.\nThis assumes that the host of the patch (most likely Resolume Arena or Avenue), is keeping up with the beat.\n\nThe BPM in the transport bar in Wire is just a dummy to test with.\nWhen running your patch in another application like Resolume Arena, the patch will use that application's BPM instead.\n\nMake sure to hit the play button in the transport bart to get the transport going!\nPro Tip: use the SHIFT + ENTER shortcut to quickly start\/pause the transport"
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 339.462615966796875,
					"width": 812.71673583984375,
					"x": -42.952423095703125,
					"y": -115.37241363525390625
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffd0c117",
				"constants": {},
